  <section class="feat rev" id="together">
    <div class="wrap feat-in">
      <div class="feat-copy" data-reveal>
        <h2>Two machines, one timeline</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          A six-character code puts both players on the same position, the same pauses and the same
          episode. Seek on one and the other lands on the same frame — the room's state travels, not
          the gesture, so a message arriving late still says where playback should be now.
        </p>
        <ul class="checks">
          <li><svg class="ic" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><use href="#i-check"/></svg>
            <span><b>Drift is corrected by speed, never by seeking.</b> A seek to fix a tenth of a second costs a decode from the previous keyframe and lands further out than it started; ten seconds at 103% erases it inaudibly.</span></li>
          <li><svg class="ic" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><use href="#i-check"/></svg>
            <span><b>The room waits for whoever is still buffering</b> and resumes by itself when they are ready.</span></li>
          <li><svg class="ic" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><use href="#i-check"/></svg>
            <span><b>If it is a torrent, nobody pastes anything</b> — everyone fetches from the same swarm and follows you through the season. Nothing but the timeline goes through the relay; no media ever does.</span></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
